    "It begins with the young man, because everything Augustine became is a reaction against who he was. In his twenties he was a star: a brilliant, ferociously ambitious provincial from a small town in Roman North Africa, climbing the one ladder a clever poor boy could climb in the late empire, which was rhetoric, the art of speaking, the skill that won lawsuits and imperial appointments and made a teacher rich. He was good enough at it to teach it in Carthage, then to be handed the rhetoric chair in Milan, where the emperor's court actually sat. He had a long-term partner he never married and a son he loved. And he was sure, the way only a clever young man can be sure, that Christianity was a religion for simpletons: crude stories, a crude God, a book whose prose embarrassed a man with taste.",
