      "The tradition that came before deserves its strongest case, because Augustine loved it and owed it everything. Ancient philosophy was, overwhelmingly, an *outward* search. The pre-Socratics asked what the cosmos was made of. Plato sent the mind climbing *up*, out of the cave, toward Forms that hang (so to speak) above the visible world: eternal, public, impersonal, the same for everyone who reaches them. The Stoics studied the rational order running through all of nature and tried to fall in line with it. Even the inwardness the Platonists did have (Plotinus, the third-century thinker whose books would crack Augustine open, taught a turn inward and an ascent to the One) was a turn inward in order to climb *out* of the self toward something impersonal and universal, a One that is nobody, that waits for no one, reached by intellect and discipline if one has them. The good life was a public project of the reasoning mind, pointed at an order out there in the structure of things. This is a serious, beautiful tradition, and nothing here is a strawman: it gave Augustine his metaphysics and very nearly his soul.",
