          "p": "And the move that earns him real respect is that he names where it's weak. The privation theory has a famous, hard objection, and Augustine's heirs argue about it to this day, so it deserves full force. Take a torturer, someone who delights in another's agony, who is *good at* cruelty, inventive, attentive, fully engaged. Is that monstrousness really just an *absence*, a hole, a nothing? It feels like the most *present*, most positive thing in the room. Saying \"the torturer's cruelty is merely the lack of the love that should be there\" can sound like a bookkeeping trick played on something that is obviously, actively real. Augustine's answer (and whether it satisfies is a matter readers still divide on) is to hold the line: yes, the energy, the cleverness, the attention are all real goods, gifts of a good nature, and they are real precisely because they exist; what makes the act *evil* is not any of that positive equipment but the thing that is *missing* from it, the love and justice that ought to be steering it and aren't. The cruelty is the privation made active, good capacities harnessed to a corrupt order of loves. Whether that fully accounts for the felt *positivity* of malice is one of the live arguments in the philosophy of religion, and it has been since he wrote. But the mark of a real philosopher is in what he does next: he hands over the sharpest knife pointed at his own theory rather than hiding it. He found the crack and showed it. (Plato did exactly this to his own Forms; the move is the mark.)"
